    "imageCredit": "AI-generated editorial illustration; not a documentary photograph.",
    "body": [
      [
        "The runway is a signal, not a shopping list",
        "A runway is designed to stop you in your tracks. Your wardrobe has to get you through an actual day: the early meeting, the walk across town, the jacket you leave on the back of a chair. Treat fashion week as a source of ideas, not a demand to replace the clothes that already feel like you.\n\nSpring/Summer 2027 coverage has repeatedly pointed to lighter, more fluid dressing and a stronger appetite for individual expression. Designers showed skirts in many forms, sheer and airy layers, and unexpected combinations; reporters also noted practical tailoring and versatile pieces alongside the spectacle [1][2]. Those are ingredients, not instructions.\n\nThe difference matters. A designer makes a creative argument; no runway can predict what every person will want to wear six months from now. Take the part that catches your eye and leave the rest."
      ],
      [
        "Three ideas worth taking home",
        "First, ease has shape. A jacket can be relaxed without looking careless when the shoulder, sleeve and length make sense. Second, texture does work. A woven, pleated or tactile piece can give a quiet outfit depth without adding loud color. Third, contrast is more interesting than a matching set: a precise coat over an open collar, a soft knit with a harder trouser, a clean dress with one piece of substantial jewelry.\n\nThese are editorial observations from the season's reporting, not rules handed down by a fashion council. Try the one that fits your own taste. Leave the rest on the runway.\n\nFor a man who usually reaches for dark denim and a shirt, the move might be a softer unlined jacket, a knit polo or one unexpected texture. For a woman who prefers quiet tailoring, it could be a fluid skirt with a sharper boot, or a lighter fabric under a structured coat. Neither outfit needs to borrow the other person’s silhouette. Both can share a mood—ease, precision, a little drama—without becoming a couple costume."
      ],
      [
        "A wardrobe needs a point of view",
        "Before you buy anything, name the feeling you want your clothes to carry. Composed? Athletic? Romantic? Uncomplicated? That single word becomes a filter. If the item only works with a fantasy calendar, it is a costume rental waiting to happen.\n\nLook at what you already wear most. The repeat pieces reveal your actual proportions and habits: how much structure you like, what shoes your days require, whether you reach for layers, and which fabrics you can live in. Build around those truths, then add one new element with intention.\n\nFabric earns its keep in motion. Sit down, reach overhead, walk a block and check whether the piece still feels like yours. If you can, look at care instructions before buying; a beautiful garment that needs a lifestyle of its own may not be a useful addition. Small alterations can make a familiar jacket look more intentional than a new one bought in a hurry.\n\nThree real-life uniforms are enough to make the ideas useful. For a training day that turns into lunch, try a fitted tee, clean technical trouser, overshirt and a shoe you would happily wear indoors. For dinner, change the tee for a knit or open-collar shirt, switch to a sharper trouser and keep one strong accessory. For travel, start with comfortable layers that work together: one jacket, two tops, one trouser, one shoe you can walk in. The point is not a capsule wardrobe formula; it is fewer pieces that earn their place across more than one scene."
      ],
      [
        "Make trend personal",
        "A 2027 runway idea can be tested without purchasing a whole new wardrobe. Borrow the silhouette from a jacket you own. Try a softer layer under sharper tailoring. Use a scarf, belt or textured bag as the accent. If the proportions feel wrong, change the proportions; the trend does not get to overrule the person wearing it.\n\nFor couples, sharing a style language does not mean dressing as a set. Agree on the occasion or mood, then let each person interpret it in their own way. One chooses shape; the other chooses texture. Coordination is a conversation, not a uniform."
      ],
      [
        "The ten-minute edit",
        "Choose one outfit you wore last month and try it again with a single deliberate change: adjust the hem, switch the shoe, add a layer, remove one accessory, or make the fit cleaner. Take a photograph in daylight. If you feel more like yourself, keep the combination. If not, you have learned something without buying anything.\n\nLuxury is often mistaken for price. In practice, it is attention: fabric that feels good, clothes that fit, care that keeps them sharp, and a point of view that belongs to you. Wear the trend if it serves that. The point is to be seen clearly, not to look like the feed.\n\nA simple style test: does the piece work with three things you already own, and would you wear it on an ordinary Tuesday? If yes, the trend has become yours. If it only works in a photograph, admire the photograph. Your closet is not a museum of runway references."
      ]
    ],
